{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Let now a little be taken, etc.</b> Perhaps he did not wish to deviate from the custom of the ancients regarding guests, who would wash their feet first out of concern for idolatrous worship, as they of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a ch. 6). And his saying <b>a little</b> — this was his custom, to say to guests, to show that there is not much bother upon them, so as not to embarrass them." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes to a matter touching the angels, that they should take a little water — which is the Torah. For the Torah has an aspect of peshat (surface meaning) and an aspect of penimi'ut (inner meaning). Now the angels who came in the likeness of men — this is a body that HaShem created for them from the condensation of the airs (Zohar vol. 1, 58a), and relative to the essence of the angel it is considered a body, and is attributed to it an aspect of רגל (leg), as is known to those who know חן. And he hinted to them that they should take a little water from the peshat-level of the Torah and purify with it their רגליהם (feet), which is the aspect of the body that is visible upon them. <b>And recline under the tree</b> — which is the Torah, which is called עץ חיים (Tree of Life); therefore he said העץ (the tree) with the definite article. He also told them to take <b>a piece of bread</b>, which is the aspect of the penimi'ut of the Torah, and to sustain with it their innermost being — and this is his saying ״לִבְּכֶם״ (to your heart). And he was precise in saying <b>פַּת לֶחֶם</b> (a piece of bread) — a piece in which there is the aspect of לֶחֶם (bread), which are three havayot (existences/divine vitalities), which are the aspect of the חַיּוּת (vitality) of the worlds."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:54.984263517Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}